184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (The field of autonomous floor cleaning robotics has seen significant progression, transitioning from purely random path execution to intelligent, sensor-driven systems. Researchers have explored various control paradigms, navigation algorithms, and mechanical designs to maximize dust collection efficiency while minimizing cost and power overhead. This section evaluates recent methodologies implemented in microcontroller-based cleaning systems.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (An autonomous vacuum cleaning prototype developed by K. Sarath Kumar et al. [a] focuses on spatial evaluation using pre-programmed decision logic on an Arduino platform. When the vehicle encounters an obstruction, its sensor loop determines the direction with optimal boundary clearance and commands the drivetrain to turn toward that open space. The physical design relies on a lithium battery configuration driving a high-RPM axial fan coupled directly to a motor. To measure physical operational parameters, the authors incorporated an anemometer and a pressure gauge, concluding that a detachable collection bag improves overall suction efficiency. Meanwhile, long-distance communication methods were introduced by Humayun Rashid et al. [b] through the implementation of a Dual-Tone Multi-Frequency \(DTMF\) control architecture. This mechanism allows a cell phone to manipulate the room cleaner from unrestricted distances over cellular frequencies. The robot operates via two distinct smart path-following methods to ensure complete area coverage, and features an upgraded vacuum system equipped with specific filtration units to manage airflow safely.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (To bridge the gap between localized control and automation, K.A.M. Annuar et al. [c] integrated an HC-06 Bluetooth module with an Arduino controller, creating an interface that links seamlessly with an Android smartphone. This structure offers dual operational flexibility, letting the user toggle between a self-directed autonomous mode and a manual mode where movement is directed in real time via a custom mobile application. In contrast to manual orientation, P. Pravin et al. [d] targeted algorithmic path efficiency by introducing a border analysis technique. Instead of traversing a room arbitrarily, this high-pressure automatic vacuum robot maps out and traverses the boundaries of an area first, subsequently adopting a systematic zigzag trajectory to cover the remaining floor space. The system analyzes obstacles using an array of ultrasonic sensors and cleans using a specialized high-pressure suction pump. Further refining the hybrid control scheme, D. Desmulyati et al. [e] designed an Arduino R3-based autonomous vacuum cleaner capable of dynamic obstacle avoidance. Their system pairs localized smartphone control via Bluetooth with a physical, chassis-mounted analog ON/OFF push button, providing an immediate manual override option for safety and operational testing.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
